Hi Neal,

Quick heads-up on the MicroBlaze entry.S argument-save-area fix (the
"GCC 15 regression"). I had the bot write and test a kselftest for it, in
case we want a regression test to go alongside the patch. It's ready if we
need it -- no pressure to use it. I also had it run code coverage over the
patched entry.S so we can say the fix is actually exercised, not just
asserted. Summary below; patches attached.

=== The test (attached: 0002, a standalone kselftest) ===

tools/testing/selftests/microblaze/entry_abi.c. It asserts the one property
the fix restores: a syscall does not change the user stack pointer. It reads
r1 immediately before and after the "brki r14, 8" trap in a single asm
block, so the compiler can't interpose stack motion. Standard kselftest TAP
output, arch-gated (skips on non-MicroBlaze).

Verified both directions:
  - fixed kernel: "ok 1 user stack pointer preserved across ... syscalls".
  - stock entry.S, same GCC 15: the test's own faccessat(AT_FDCWD, ...)
    syscall corrupts the saved SP -- the fault dump shows r1 = 0xFFFFFF9C,
    which is AT_FDCWD (-100), i.e. the syscall's first argument written over
    the stack pointer -- and the process dies on it.

Honest caveat: because this bug corrupts the SP of any process that makes a
spilling syscall, a reverted kernel can't reach a usable userspace at all
(init panics), so there's no graceful "not ok" to print -- the failure
signal is "doesn't boot / no ok emitted", which CI records as a failure.
That's as strong as a total-boot-failure bug allows.

=== Code coverage of the fix ===

I ran the patched entry.S under a QEMU TCG coverage plugin with one
userspace stress binary that drives each kernel-entry path, and checked
that every argument-save-area reserve site the patch adds actually executes.

Result: 14 of 14 reachable reserve sites executed in a single boot.

  covered  do_syscall_trace_enter      (ptrace PTRACE_SYSCALL)
  covered  syscall dispatch (bra r12)   (every syscall)
  covered  do_syscall_trace_leave      (ptrace)
  covered  do_notify_resume  x4         (signals via setitimer)
  covered  schedule_tail     x2         (fork)
  covered  full_exception               (privileged mts from user)
  covered  do_page_fault (data + instr) (demand fault + bad jump)
  covered  do_IRQ                        (timer)
  covered  sw_exception                 (brki r16, 0x18 breakpoint)

The 15th site, microblaze_kgdb_break, is inside #ifdef CONFIG_KGDB (off in
this config), so it's absent from the build -- reported as N/A, not a miss.

Two things the coverage surfaced that reading the diff wouldn't:
  - five of the sites (the exception/interrupt entry paths) execute in real
    mode at the physical alias (0x90000000), not the virtual 0xc0000000
    base -- so a coverage/filter setup has to span both.
  - per-instruction coverage is required: the reserve "addik r1,r1,-32"
    sits mid-translation-block, so TB-entry-only coverage never records it.

Net: every save-area reservation the patch adds is exercised by a real
workload, and the fix boots to a shell on the failing config on both QEMU
and hardware. (v4 and v5 both boot hardware now -- the earlier garbage
console was a different incompatible patch in that stack, not the fix.)

The patches attached are a v6 split: 1/2 is the fix (unchanged from v5),
2/2 is the test, so the test can be reviewed/applied independently. Nothing
sent to any list yet.

Cheers,
Sam

