From 03233d2ef8e29cb8370fe30105628de48a26aff3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 06:07:34 -0400
Subject: [PATCH v6 1/2] microblaze: reserve the ABI argument save area when
 calling C from entry.S

The MicroBlaze ABI has the caller reserve stack space for the arguments
it passes in registers, and permits the callee to spill its incoming
argument registers there.  GCC states this in the backend:
REG_PARM_STACK_SPACE is MAX_ARGS_IN_REGISTERS * UNITS_PER_WORD and
FIRST_PARM_OFFSET is UNITS_PER_WORD, so a callee may write to
[caller_sp + 4, caller_sp + 28).

arch/microblaze assembly calls C at seventeen sites with r1 pointing at
live saved state, so that writable area aliases data the kernel needs
back.  Fatally, one of those sites is entry.S's syscall dispatch:

	lwi	r12, r12, sys_call_table
	addi	r15, r0, ret_from_trap-8
	bra	r12

Every syscall handler is entered with its first-argument spill slot
aliasing pt_regs + 4, which asm-offsets.h names PT_R1: the saved user
stack pointer.

Until GCC 14 this was latent: the register allocator preferred
callee-saved registers for incoming arguments, so the spill slot went
unused.  GCC 15 commit 3b9b8d6cfdf5 ("ira: Scale save/restore costs of
callee save registers with block frequency") made callee-saved registers
expensive, and handlers now spill their arguments.  A kernel built with
GCC 15 corrupts PT_R1 on the first syscall whose handler does so:
init's first *at syscall overwrites the saved user SP with AT_FDCWD
(-100), and init takes SIGSEGV on its first stack access after syscall
return:

  pid=3D1 comm=3Dinit addr=3D0000003c code=3D1
   r1=3DFFFFFF9C ... rPC=3D480293BC  msr=3D000053A2 ear=3D0000003C esr=3D0000=
1012

(register dump from a show_regs() instrumentation of do_page_fault;
esr =3D data TLB miss in a delay slot.)

An objdump sweep of a GCC 15 vmlinux found 11239 functions storing
through r1 into their caller's argument area.  The idiom is ubiquitous
and correct for C callers, which always reserve the area; only the
asm-to-C boundaries violate the contract.  Beyond entry.S the audit
found the same defect live in two more places in this very binary:
hw_exception_handler.S's unaligned fixup path calls bad_page_fault,
which stores r6 and r7 at caller_sp+4 and +12 -- PT_R1 and PT_R3 -- and
head.S calls the six-argument machine_early_init with r1 four bytes
below the top of the init stack, so its spills of r6, r9 and r10 land
past the end of the stack on every boot.  ret_from_fork's call to
schedule_tail(prev) with r1 at the child's pt_regs is the same bug
waiting on the allocator's mood.

Reserve the area at each call site by lowering r1 by
C_ARG_SIZE (32: a link word plus six argument words, rounded for stack
alignment) across the call and restoring it afterwards.  Where the
return convention is r15 =3D label-8 the callee returns to the label
directly, so those sites return through a local trampoline that
restores r1 first.  sys_rt_sigreturn_wrapper derives its pt_regs
pointer from r1 and is adjusted for the reservation.  head.S instead
reserves the area once, when it first sets up the init stack.  Calls
that pass no arguments (schedule, preempt_schedule_irq, mmu_init,
start_kernel) cannot spill an incoming argument and are unchanged.

This was reported as a GCC 15 regression, and Buildroot carries a GCC
patch (a TARGET_CALLEE_SAVE_COST hook returning 1) that masks it by
suppressing the register-allocation behaviour that exposes it.  The
compiler is behaving within the ABI; that workaround can be dropped
once this is applied.

Verified on qemu_microblazeel_mmu (petalogix-s3adsp1800) with an A/B on
identical Linux 6.12.81 trees and gated compiler provenance:

  GCC 15.3.0, stock kernel:      hang after "Run /init as init process"
  GCC 15.3.0, this patch:        boots to shell, no user faults
  GCC 15.3.0 + the Buildroot GCC workaround, stock kernel: boots

An intermediate version covering every site except the syscall dispatch
moved the failure from a hang to SIGSEGV in the signal path, as the
mechanism predicts.

Kernel stack cost is 32 bytes per asm-to-C call while it is in flight.

Link: https://gcc.gnu.org/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=3D121432
Reported-by: Thomas Petazzoni <thomas.petazzoni@bootlin.com>
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org # all trees built with GCC >=3D 15
Signed-off-by: Sam Price <thesamprice@gmail.com>
Assisted-by: Claude (Anthropic)
---

v4 -> v5: fixes a real regression v4 introduced, found by Sam Price on
hardware: after the first user-mode unaligned access, userspace produced
garbage console output.  v4 wrapped the call to
_unaligned_data_exception as if it were a C function.  It is assembly
(hw_exception_handler.S), it makes no C calls, it never reads r1 -- and
it exits with a direct brai ret_from_exc, bypassing the restore
trampoline, so ret_from_exc rebuilt the register file from pt_regs
minus the reservation and returned corrupted state to user space.  The
site is reverted to pristine, with a comment explaining why it must not
get the C-callee treatment.  The other exception callees were
re-verified as genuine asmlinkage C (full_exception, sw_exception,
do_page_fault); those wraps stand.  Site count corrected to seventeen.
QEMU boots never exercised the unaligned path, which is why 22 runs
missed it; the failing platform reproduces it immediately and is the
definitive test.

v3 -> v4: three rted sites restored after a stale-diff omission.
v2 -> v3: regenerated as modifications rather than file creations.
v1 -> v2: the r15-rule audit added the sites beyond entry.S.

Evidence: https://github.com/thesamprice/rtems-toolchain-analysis/pull/1

